$DIR/main.p:22:14: v is dropped at the end of its block here, but a view of it (v, line 22) was stored in keepAny at line 22, where the compiler cannot check its uses
    An ordinary interface made from a map or channel in a local variable holds
    the variable's word without a count of its own (section 7.9), so it may be
    used only while the variable keeps that map or channel. The compiler follows
    views through local variables and their fields, and into the arguments of
    the calls that store them there, but the view of v made by v at line 22 went
    into memory it does not follow, so it cannot check that the view is not used
    after line 22.
    Go keeps memory alive for as long as any pointer refers to it. Plutonium has
    no garbage collector: an owner releases its memory when it is dropped, so a
    view must not be used after the value it looks at has gone.
    Keep the view in a local variable or a field of one, where the compiler
    follows it, or do not let its block end while the view is kept
    (see "Views" in the Plutonium spec, section 18.3)
    (run "pluto explain E3314" for a longer explanation)

$DIR/main.p:29:2: cannot pass U[*Inner].P(&in) to store, which keeps it after returning
    U[*Inner].P(&in) points into in. in is a local variable of f (declared at
    main.p:28). store stores u in package variable keepAny (main.p:22), so
    U[*Inner].P(&in) would outlive in.
    Go would move this memory to the heap, and its garbage collector would free
    it later. Plutonium has no garbage collector and never moves memory to the
    heap behind the program's back, so that every allocation is visible in the
    code.
    If store should keep its own copy of in, change it to take the value, not a
    pointer, or an owner it can keep: an owned[Inner] from new, a shared[Inner],
    or a library owner such as a vec.Vec.
    (see "Views" in the Plutonium spec, section 18.3)
    (run "pluto explain E3003" for a longer explanation)
